I have some contracts for ArrayList.Test where I have defined some test cases for Add, Remove, Insert, Set and Get methods of ArrayList class. It seems there are a few points which the Pex should be checked. Please advise how can I test these as per the requirements specified in my contracts. 
Also I am new in programming so if you can be more descriptive when you explain what each section of the code does it helps me learn.
Thanks in advance.

A: In your current code you are using the PexObserve.ValueForViewing method, which is used to generate output during test run. The main purpose here is to observe the values of variables to see any changes in their behaviors during different test executions.

However, in contracts or precondition and postcondition sections we simply check if the preconditions are met and the postconditions are held. We use Assert statements for this. 

In your case, we are just checking assumptions related to the precondition of the method. If the precondition holds true, then the execution of the test method should occur; otherwise, it doesn't belong to the scope so it should be ignored by the test executor.

In your contract-based testing, for all of the contracts in your code, we would have to replace the existing `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)` statements with assumptions related to the specific contract. The assumptions should be written in terms of the expected behavior of method under test.

For example, in the `PUT_AddContract` where you are testing the `Add` method, the `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)` statement should be replaced with: "Assume that when the integer `x` is added to the list `arrList`, the Count of the list should increase by 1". Similarly, for the other contracts such as `PUT_RemoveContract`, `PUT_InsertContract`, `PUT_SetContract`, `PUT_GetContract`, `PUT_ContainsContract`, and `PUT_ClearContract`.

If you want to actually run the tests and observe the results and values of the ArrayList throughout the test execution, you can use a third-party plugin like PexLoader or Visual Studio’s test agent which integrates with Pex and gives you an extra level of view to the values and progress of the tests. 

